NNAAMMEE
     ppmmccaannnnoottaattee — sources printout with inlined profiling

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppmmccaannnnoottaattee [--aa] [--hh] [--kk _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e] [--ll _l_e_v_e_l] [--mm _m_o_d_e] _p_m_c_o_u_t_._o_u_t
                 _b_i_n_a_r_y_o_b_j

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppmmccaannnnoottaattee utility can produce both C sources or assembly sources of
     a program with a line-by-line based profiling.  The profiling information
     is retrieved through a pmcstat(8) raw output while the program operations
     are retrieved through the objdump(1) tool.

     When calling ppmmccaannnnoottaattee the raw output is passed through the _p_m_c_o_u_t_._o_u_t
     argument, while the program is passed through the _b_i_n_a_r_y_o_b_j argument.

     As long as ppmmccaannnnoottaattee relies on objdump(1) and pmcstat(8) to work, it
     will fail if one of them is not available.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     The following options are available:

     --aa      Shows the program profiling inlined in the assembly code only.
             No C information involving C sources is provided.

     --hh      Prints out information about the usage of the tool.

     --ll _l_e_v_e_l
             Changes the lower bound (expressed in percentage) for traced
             functions that will be printed out in the report.  The default
             value is 0.5%.

     --kk _k_e_r_n_e_l_d_i_r
             Set the pathname of the kernel directory to argument _k_e_r_n_e_l_d_i_r.
             This directory specifies where ppmmccaannnnoottaattee should look for the
             kernel and its modules.  The default is _/_b_o_o_t_/_k_e_r_n_e_l.

     --mm _m_o_d_e
             Format to use when annotating source or assembly lines.  One of:

             bblloocckk   Display percentage of matching samples relative to sam‐
                     ples falling in the current block.

             gglloobbaall  Display percentage of matching samples relative to all
                     samples.

             rraaww     Display the raw count of matching samples.

LLIIMMIITTAATTIIOONNSS
     As long as ppmmccaannnnoottaattee relies on the objdump(1) utility to retrieve the C
     code, the program needs to be compiled with debugging options.  Some‐
     times, in particular with heavy optimization levels, the objdump(1) util‐
     ity embeds the code of inlining functions directly in the callers, making
     an output difficult to read.  The x86 version reports the sampling from
     pmcstat collecting the following instruction in regard of the interrupted
     one.  This means that the samples may be attributed to the line below the
     one of interest.

